    <div class="container relative z-10">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="text-white/80 mb-3">July 9, 2026 • 11 min read</p>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-4">
          How to Move Into Older Historic Homes in Denver With Narrow Hallways and Staircases
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-white/90">
          Denver's historic neighborhoods are full of beautiful older homes — and tight staircases, narrow doorways, and hallways that simply weren't built for modern sectional sofas. Here's how to approach a move into one of these properties without damaging the home or your belongings.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Article Content -->
  <article class="py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-lg">

        <p class="lead">
          Denver's older neighborhoods are some of the most desirable in the city. Capitol Hill, Potter-Highlands, West Highland, Platt Park, Whittier, Curtis Park — these are blocks of century-old homes with character that no new construction can replicate. Original hardwood floors, detailed woodwork, high ceilings, sleeping porches, and layouts that reflect a different era of how people lived. They're also, in many cases, genuinely difficult to move into.
        </p>

        <p>
          Homes built in the early 1900s weren't designed around a sectional sofa, a California King mattress, or a 65-inch TV. Stairwells that are technically functional for people are sometimes barely wide enough for a single dresser turned at exactly the right angle. Doorways that accommodate a standard door don't always accommodate furniture without some creative problem-solving. And hardwood floors that are 100 years old don't forgive carelessness.
        </p>

        <p>
          Moving into these homes is absolutely doable — it just requires the right preparation and the right crew. This guide walks through what makes historic Denver properties different and how to approach the move properly.
        </p>

        <h2>Measure Everything Before Move Day — Not During</h2>

        <p>
          The single most preventable problem in a historic home move is discovering on move day that something doesn't fit. A couch that's an inch too wide for the stairwell landing, a dining table that won't make the turn from the front door into the dining room, or a bedroom dresser that can't get past the bathroom doorframe — these are problems that can be anticipated and planned for in advance.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before your move, take measurements of the following at your new home:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Front door width (frame to frame) and height</li>
          <li>Hallway width at the narrowest point</li>
          <li>Stairwell width and any landing dimensions where turns are required</li>
          <li>Ceiling height on each floor, particularly on the stairs</li>
          <li>Interior doorway widths for each room you're moving furniture into</li>
          <li>Any tight corners between the entry and the primary bedroom or living area</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Then measure your largest furniture pieces: sectional sofa sections, bed frames, mattresses (which flex but only so much), dressers, wardrobe armoires, and any other large items. Compare them to the path they'll need to travel through. For anything that's close — within two or three inches on any dimension — flag it as potentially problematic before move day so the crew can plan for it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Many people skip this step because measuring feels tedious. It's an hour of your time that can prevent a half-day problem on move day.
        </p>

        <h2>Denver's Historic Staircases: What You're Actually Dealing With</h2>

        <p>
          The staircases in Denver's older homes were built to a different standard. Typical dimensions for early 20th century construction included:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Stairwell widths as narrow as 32–36 inches (versus the 48-inch minimum in contemporary construction)</li>
          <li>Steeper rise-to-run ratios, meaning the steps are both taller and shorter in depth than modern stairs</li>
          <li>90-degree landings, often with very limited landing space before turning</li>
          <li>Low overhead clearance at the top or bottom of the staircase, particularly in bungalows and Victorian-era homes</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          These characteristics don't make a move impossible — they make it a puzzle that requires planning. An experienced moving crew knows how to angle furniture, how to use moving straps for controlled carries up steep stairs, and when something genuinely needs to be disassembled versus when it can be angled through with the right technique.
        </p>

        <p>
          What makes this different from a move into a newer construction home is that experience matters more. A crew that's worked in Denver's historic neighborhoods before has encountered these configurations. They know that a 36-inch stairwell with an L-shaped landing can accommodate a queen mattress but not a California King flat — the King goes up on its side with a pivot at the landing. They know that a wingback armchair that can't go through a 32-inch doorframe can usually go through a window opening if there's access to one. These are not tricks you want someone figuring out for the first time at your expense.
        </p>

        <h2>Floor Protection Is Not Optional in Historic Homes</h2>

        <p>
          The hardwood floors in a historic Denver home are often original — sometimes refinished once or twice, sometimes not at all. They're one of the features that makes these homes valuable, and they're also vulnerable to the heavy, abrasive traffic of a move.
        </p>

        <p>
          Any reputable moving company will use floor protection as a standard part of the job. This typically includes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ram board or heavy contractor paper laid down the primary travel path before any furniture moves</li>
          <li>Moving blankets on door jambs, railings, and wall corners that furniture will pass near</li>
          <li>Felt pads under furniture legs before anything is set down</li>
          <li>Furniture dollies with soft rubber wheels that don't mar floors, used only on protected surfaces</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          When you're vetting moving companies for a historic home move, ask specifically how they approach floor and wall protection. If the answer is vague or it seems like an afterthought, that's meaningful information. In an older home with original woodwork and hardwood floors, protection is part of the job, not an upgrade.
        </p>

        <h2>Disassembly: When It Makes Sense and When It Doesn't</h2>

        <p>
          Some furniture that can't navigate a historic home's narrow passages in its assembled state can go in pieces. This is worth thinking through before move day rather than deciding under pressure:
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Bed frames</strong> almost always disassemble and almost always should for a staircase move. Even a queen bed frame that would theoretically fit up narrow stairs is easier, safer, and faster to move disassembled.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sectional sofas</strong> separate at the connecting hardware. The individual sections are significantly easier to navigate than the assembled piece. If you have a large sectional and a narrow staircase, the sections should be separated before the movers arrive.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Dressers and wardrobes</strong> are often lighter and more maneuverable with drawers removed. The drawers can be carried separately and the carcass is easier to angle through doorways.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Where disassembly gets complicated</strong> is with furniture that doesn't easily reassemble or that might not hold together properly after being taken apart. Older pieces, veneer furniture, and items with glued joints rather than mechanical fasteners need to be assessed carefully before assuming they can be disassembled safely.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're not sure about a specific piece, the best approach is to flag it when you request your quote so the crew knows it needs to be assessed during the pre-move walkthrough. A <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/get-quote">quote conversation</a> is the right time to surface these specifics.
        </p>

        <h2>The Window Option for Truly Impossible Situations</h2>

        <p>
          Occasionally, there is simply no interior path for a piece of furniture. A grand piano on the second floor of a historic home, a large armoire in a third-floor bedroom with a staircase that turns twice — some situations don't have an interior solution.
        </p>

        <p>
          In these cases, the window or exterior access option is worth considering. This involves removing a window sash (which can be done without damaging the window in most cases), using straps and a controlled exterior lift to get the piece up to the correct floor level, and bringing it in through the opening. It requires planning, the right equipment, and crew experience — but it's a real option for pieces that genuinely cannot go through a historic home's interior.
        </p>

        <p>
          This isn't something to coordinate on the fly. If you suspect a piece might need window access, mention it when you book and have that conversation in advance.
        </p>

        <h2>Parking and Access in Denver's Older Neighborhoods</h2>

        <p>
          The neighborhoods where Denver's historic homes are concentrated — Capitol Hill, <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/cities/denver">central Denver</a>, West Highland, and the area north of downtown — were platted before large moving trucks existed. The street widths, the parking situation, and the distance from where a truck can legally park to your front door vary significantly from block to block.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before your move, look at the street in front of your new home:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Is there space for a large truck to park within a reasonable distance of your front door?</li>
          <li>Are there parking restrictions on the street (street cleaning, residential permit zones, time limits)?</li>
          <li>Is there an alley behind the home that might provide better access than the front?</li>
          <li>If parking is limited, can you reserve space with parking cones or temporary no-parking permits from the City of Denver?</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Denver does allow residents to request temporary no-parking zones for moves with some advance notice. This is particularly valuable on narrow Capitol Hill or West Highland streets where a moving truck parked in the travel lane creates significant issues. The process involves contacting the city's right-of-way management office. Your moving company may have done this before and can point you in the right direction.
        </p>

        <h2>Communicate the Details With Your Moving Crew Upfront</h2>

        <p>
          A historic home move goes better when the crew isn't discovering the property's constraints on move day. Whatever you know about the home — the narrow stairwell, the tricky landing, the dining room doorway that's only 28 inches, the original hardwood that needs protection — should be communicated when you book, not during the walk-through on move day.
        </p>

        <p>
          The more specific you are, the better the crew can prepare. An experienced mover who knows they're walking into a 1908 bungalow in the Platt Park neighborhood with a 34-inch stairwell and a California King bed can think through that problem before arriving. The same mover who shows up expecting a standard job and encounters it for the first time at 8am is starting from scratch.
        </p>

        <p>
          For <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/services/residential-moving">residential moving</a> in Denver's established neighborhoods, the specifics matter. Share them.
        </p>

        <h2>What to Ask When Choosing a Mover for a Historic Home</h2>

        <p>
          Not every moving company has meaningful experience in Denver's older housing stock. When you're getting quotes, a few questions worth asking:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Have you moved into or out of homes in this neighborhood before?</li>
          <li>How do you handle a staircase that's 34 inches wide with a landing turn?</li>
          <li>What floor and wall protection do you bring as standard?</li>
          <li>What happens if a piece of furniture genuinely can't make it through the interior path?</li>
          <li>Do you carry tools for basic furniture disassembly?</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The answers tell you whether you're talking to someone who knows this type of work or someone who's going to figure it out when they get there. For a historic home, that distinction matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can also check the <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/reviews">reviews</a> for any company you're considering — people who've moved into older Denver homes tend to mention it specifically when a crew handled the access challenges well (or didn't).
        </p>

        <h2>After the Move: Settling Into a Historic Home</h2>

        <p>
          Once the truck leaves, there's usually a brief period of figuring out that a few things didn't end up quite where you planned. That's normal. Historic homes have a way of reshaping how you arrange furniture simply because the layout reflects a different time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give yourself a week before committing to any furniture arrangement. Live in the space with what you have and let the natural flow of the rooms show you where things actually belong. The kitchen table might work better in the breakfast nook than the formal dining room. The bedroom arrangement you planned might not account for which direction the morning light comes in. Historic homes have personalities; let the personality show you something.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're planning a move into one of Denver's older neighborhoods and want to talk through the specifics — neighborhood access, staircase constraints, furniture that might need special handling — <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/contact">reaching out directly</a> before you book is always a reasonable first step. The <a href="https://www.legacymovingdenver.com/faqs">FAQ page</a> covers general questions, but the specifics of your property are worth a direct conversation.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
